> API and interpretation

The native sign-alignment convention avoids alternate displayed signs for
negative dominant eigenvalues. A tiny product stops rather than inventing a
normalized zero direction; it is distinct from a general convergence
certificate. Nonsymmetric inputs are accepted as finite square matrices but the
symmetric spectral-gap discussion is not promised for all of them. Counters
include the final product used by the actual Rayleigh value and residual, but
exclude dot-product and residual scalar work. Finite inputs can still overflow
arithmetic and then fail explicitly.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Matrix Computations, Golub and Van Loan|https://www.press.jhu.edu/books/title/10678/matrix-computations]

Verified in the shared ten-document batch: 106 algorithm CTest cases plus90
graphics cases;21 directly instrumented algorithm ASan/UBSan cases and renderer
SVG/PNG sanitizer probe;119 executable public examples;587/587 scoped
definitions plus9 changed legend definitions;10 hovers;56287 independent
records/278204 checks;frozen compatibility for all ten algorithm families;13
checkout/installed standalone headers and linked consumer;40 executed
programs/40 reviewed figures with both exports. The legacy source is retired
only after these gates pass.

Residuals across different matrices need not rank angular errors: as the second
diagonal eigenvalue approaches1, the matrix approaches the identity and many
directions have small residuals. The fixed-product-count panel therefore reports
actual residuals without calling them a cross-matrix direction-error ranking.
